# 03 — Efficient Editing *efficient-editing*

This tutorial covers the core editing enhancements in this
configuration, designed to speed up your daily editing workflow
significantly.

---

## Flash Jump *flash-jump*

Traditional `f`/`t` motions require you to press a character, then
use `;`/`,` to retarget. Flash replaces this with real-time
label-based jumps:

- `s` — Normal/Visual/Operator — Type a target character, get
  auto-generated labels, jump instantly
- `S` — Normal/Visual/Operator — Treesitter jump — move to function
  names, class definitions, etc.
- `r` — Operator — Remote Flash, jump across windows
- `R` — Operator/Visual — Treesitter range search
- `<C-s>` — Command-line — Toggle Flash search in cmdline mode

When you press `s` followed by a character, Flash places short labels
(usually two letters) on every occurrence of that character. Type the
label to jump there directly — no more `f` then `;` repeatedly.

TIP: Flash also works in operator-pending mode. For example, `ys`
(from vim-surround) combined with Flash lets you visually scope a
surround operation.

---

## Multicursor Editing *multicursor-editing*

When you need to edit multiple locations simultaneously, multicursor
mode is the most efficient tool:

- `<up>` — Normal/Visual — Add cursor above
- `<down>` — Normal/Visual — Add cursor below
- `<leader><up>` — Normal/Visual — Skip candidate above
- `<leader><down>` — Normal/Visual — Skip candidate below
- `<leader>n` — Normal — Select next match and add cursor
- `<leader>s` — Normal — Skip current match
- `<leader>N` — Normal — Select previous match and add cursor
- `<C-q>` — Normal/Visual — Toggle cursor manually

Typical workflow: To rename all occurrences of `foo` to `bar`, place
your cursor on the first `foo`, press `<leader>n` repeatedly to
select each match, then execute `ciwbar<Esc>` to change all at once.

---

## Code Folding (UFO) *code-folding*

UFO provides smarter code folding with support for both LSP ranges
and Treesitter-based folding:

- `zR` — Open all folds
- `zM` — Close all folds
- `zr` — Open folds incrementally
- `zm` — Close folds incrementally
- `K` — Peek fold content on a folded line; trigger LSP hover
  otherwise

Neovim's built-in `zc` (close fold) and `zo` (open fold) also work.
`K` is especially useful: on a folded line it shows a preview of the
collapsed content, while on normal code it triggers an LSP hover
window with type information and documentation.

---

## Search & Replace *search-replace*

- `<leader>fs` — Normal/Visual — Open rip-substitute interface

Enter a search pattern (supports regex), then the replacement text.
The interface shows a real-time preview of all matches and their
replacements before you commit the change. This is much safer than
`:%s/` for project-wide bulk replacements.

---

## Window Operations *window-operations*

Hydra-based window management lets you perform multiple operations
without repeating the prefix key:

- `<C-w>c` + `h/j/k/l` — Activate navigation Hydra — `h`/`l` move
  left/right, `j`/`k` up/down; press any other key to exit
- `<C-w>W` + `h/j/k/l` — Activate resize Hydra — `h`/`l` adjust
  width, `j`/`k` adjust height

Once a Hydra is active, you can press direction keys repeatedly
without repressing `<C-w>`. This makes window management much
smoother than the traditional `Ctrl-w + arrow key` approach.

---

## Quick Newline Insertion *quick-newline*

Insert blank lines without leaving normal mode:

- `<CR>` — Insert a blank line below the current line (cursor stays
  in place)
- `<S-CR>` — Insert a blank line above the current line

When you want to free up space around a block of code, move to the
target line in normal mode and press `<CR>` or `<S-CR>`.

---

## Number Increment/Decrement *number-incdec*

- `+` — Increment the number under the cursor
- `-` — Decrement the number under the cursor

Place your cursor on a number (e.g., the `5` in `count = 5`), press
`+` to get `6`, or `-` to go back to `5`. The cursor only needs to be
somewhere on the digit, not at the beginning.

---

## Undo Tree *undo-tree*

Neovim's built-in undo history is linear, but undotree displays it as
a branching tree:

- `<C-x>u` — Toggle undotree
- `<leader>u` — Same as above

The undotree panel shows all your undo branches. You can jump back to
any historical state, even if it was overwritten by subsequent edits.
This is especially useful when you accidentally undo and then make
more changes — you can still recover the intermediate state.

---

## Scenario: Refactoring Function Parameters *refactoring-scenario*

Suppose you have this code:

~~~python
def process(a, b, c):
    print(a)
    print(b)
    print(c)
~~~

And you want to rename the parameters to `x`, `y`, `z`:

1. Press `S` then type `parameter` to jump to the function definition
   (Treesitter)
2. Press `zM` to fold everything, then `zr` to unfold to a
   comfortable level
3. Place cursor on `a`, press `<leader>n` three times to select all
   three parameters
4. Execute `cwx<Esc>` to rename all cursored instances to `x`
5. Repeat for `b` → `y` and `c` → `z`
6. Made a mistake? Press `<leader>u` to open undotree and recover

